# Engineering Policy

## Contract-First Execution

- Define the Goal Contract before long-running, migration/release, broad,
  delegation-backed, or cross-context work.
- Keep required behavior in OpenSpec and execution state in `TASK_LEDGER.md`
  plus `.planning/devflow/**`.
- Matt skills provide engineering primitives only; their notes are drafts until
  promoted into an approved canonical artifact.
- Hooks remain read-only with respect to installers, dependency activation,
  project migration, release sync apply, archive, and Git operations.
- Unknown capability IDs, stale required resources, ambiguous ownership, and
  retired workflow keys fail closed.

## Project-Directed Implementation Readiness

- Create a Requirement only from an approved active plan that explicitly
  selects an external implementation provider; advisory direction and chat are
  not authority.
- Record that selection first as `implementation_readiness.required: true` in
  DevFlow state. Missing Requirement/Evidence then fails closed as Required;
  `false` means the approved plan selected no external provider.
- Bind Requirement, provider Evidence, and DevFlow Receipt to exact provider
  artifact, consumer identity/revision, active semantic plan, target profile,
  required capability set, limitations, and evaluator version.
- Require one current Ready receipt and the independent ordinary implementation
  authority before governed product mutation. Either gate may block the action.
- Any semantic/identity drift or named-human provider override invalidates the
  prior receipt. Never discover, install, activate, invoke, or silently select
  a fallback provider from this gate.

## Continuous Execution

- Default approved multi-item work to `auto-until-terminal`; a completed item,
  phase, checkpoint, review, or active change is a technical boundary rather
  than an implicit human confirmation gate.
- `execute-task` owns one item and evidence receipt. `project-orchestrator` owns
  next-item selection and continues until a genuine missing-authority gate, a
  technical fail-closed repair stop, or verified overall completion.
- Prefer the active Full OpenSpec task list for remaining-work decisions and
  fall back to `TASK_LEDGER.md` only when no active OpenSpec list exists. Never
  merge them into another queue.
- Resolve every stop through the versioned authority-delta seam. Only non-empty
  concrete `missingAuthority` may create a Human Gate; ordinary drift, failed
  validation, stage boundaries, and in-slice implementation choices do not.
- The single gate recorder writes both awaiting markers and a deterministic
  receipt together. Stop/doctor/hooks are read-only consumers and cannot invent
  or persist a gate.
- A Standing Goal Execution Authority binds exact task/provider/model,
  existing-auth credential policy, cost policy, and serial execution. A one-use
  attempt receipt is technical replay evidence, not a one-use human grant.
  Record actual monetary cost; do not create per-call or per-retry currency
  confirmation. Same-authority failure, repair, refreeze, and retry remain
  technical execution, while `DEFER_AND_CONTINUE` keeps non-blocking related
  improvements out of the critical path until the completion summary.
- Stop hooks remain read-only. They may block premature termination and report
  the continuation outcome, but they do not execute tasks, write workflow
  state, or perform side effects.

## Testing and Evidence

- Use TDD for features, bugs, refactors, business logic, and risky behavior.
- Record the observed RED failure and final GREEN result, or document why TDD
  does not apply.
- Prefer public-seam tests and characterization coverage before risky
  brownfield edits.
- Run fresh focused and broad validation before completion claims.
- Store DevFlow verification evidence below
  `.planning/devflow/verification/` and link it from the active ledger item.

## Severe Finding Human Stop

- Classify a finding as `BLOCKED_AWAITING_HUMAN` only when continuing requires a
  concrete new permission or material risk acceptance, such as an unresolved
  product/ownership decision, expanded public contract, production dependency,
  irreversible migration, undeclared external effect, credible data loss or
  authority bypass risk, or ambiguous material ownership/risk. Repairable
  evidence ambiguity remains fail-closed but is not automatically a Human Gate.
- Finish only safe read-only diagnosis, preserve the current work, and record
  evidence, impact, safe options, and a recommended decision in
  `TASK_LEDGER.md`.
- Ask the human one concrete decision. Promote the answer into OpenSpec or the
  active ledger before resuming; do not speculate, auto-resume, or perform an
  unapproved fix.
- Unknown or ambiguous severity fails closed. An unresolved severe finding
  blocks continuation, completion claims, verification readiness, and archive.

## Bounded Subagents

- Validate an Agent Task Contract before delegating implementation.
- Assign unique workers disjoint write sets; exact and parent/child overlaps are
  invalid.
- Reserve shared control-plane, OpenSpec, DevFlow state, release metadata,
  generated release, integration, and final proof for the main agent.
- Workers report commands, test logs, changed files, unverified areas, and
  residual risks, then wait for integration review.
- A worker must stop before scope expansion, dependency changes, ambiguous
  cleanup, public-contract changes, or unapproved external effects.

## Generated Artifact Safety

- A Generated Artifact Contract grants automatic cleanup authority only when
  sealed pre-creation and bound to the repository, task, run, owner, and
  command.
- Never infer or retroactively claim ownership from names, extensions, ignore
  rules, apparent cache/build purpose, or post-creation evidence.
- Prefer task/run-isolated roots. Adjacent output requires a complete
  before-state and may remove only exact paths absent from that baseline.
- A trusted orchestrator applies a fresh `AUTO_CLEAN` plan after owner exit and
  terminally verifies its receipt. Direct CLI callers still supply the explicit
  mutation safeguard. Use no wildcard,
  recursive deletion, symlink following, process kill, configuration change,
  or Git mutation.
- Automatic cleanup exclusively moves exact paths into recoverable DevFlow
  quarantine and records each mapping. Physical quarantine purge requires a
  separate Human Gate.
- Retain the immutable manifest, plan, and cleanup receipt; unresolved,
  pre-existing, tracked, protected, shared, escaped, or drifted targets stop as
  technical repair unless the resolver identifies a concrete new authority.

## Dependencies and Source Integrity

- Record repository, immutable commit, release ref, license, and file hashes for
  vendored methodology resources.
- Install or update dependencies only after explicit authorization.
- Do not let global installations satisfy project-local readiness.
- Treat secret-bearing configuration as data to inspect safely; never echo
  tokens or credentials in reports.

## Release

- Develop managed plugin and skill sources under `dev/`; generate release
  counterparts through the release promotion gate.
- For every DevFlow change, record Project Refresh Impact as `changed`,
  `verified-unchanged`, or `not-applicable` with inspected surfaces, schema
  decision, refresh-contract revision, tracked-input digest, migration/fixture
  coverage, and residual risk.
- Treat versioned project configuration targets as immutable. Configuration-
  sensitive changes require a new target, schema head, unique migration path,
  and fixtures; every refresh-sensitive byte change requires a new contract
  revision.
- Run release runtime verification, source/release parity checks, packaged
  project-refresh/fixture parity, tests, and release-target Plugin Eval before
  readiness.
- A predeclared Standing Milestone External Effects Contract may cover one exact
  reviewed commit, fast-forward-only push, immutable tag-bound publication,
  public asset readback, receipt-bound fast-forward of the named clean DevFlow
  source checkout, and named cache/current-project refresh. Its
  downstream steps reuse that standing authority and cannot prompt again.
- Archive, PR, merge, force-push, unnamed consumers/plugins, unrelated release,
  broad cleanup, and effects absent from the standing contract remain separate
  default-deny boundaries.

## Review and Completion

- Review correctness, compatibility, scope, evidence, generated artifacts,
  dependency changes, and remaining risks.
- Do not weaken or delete a current test only to hide a regression; remove a
  test when its production contract is explicitly retired and replace any
  still-relevant coverage.
- Mark a ledger item done only after its required evidence passes.
- A completion claim names exact commands/results and any residual risk.
